---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CircuitDivider from '../components/CircuitDivider.astro';
import SurfaceCodeLattice from '../components/SurfaceCodeLattice.astro';
import ThresholdChart from '../components/ThresholdChart.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';
import { url } from '../data/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Surface Code Playground"
  description="Interactive surface code demo: inject errors, read the syndrome, decode, and watch a live threshold simulation, all in the browser."
>
  <section class="container page-head">
    <p class="prompt">python -m qec.playground</p>
    <h1>Surface Code <span class="grad-text">Playground</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Qubits are fragile, so quantum computers protect information by spreading one <em>logical</em> qubit across many
      physical ones. The surface code is the leading way to do this. This page lets you break it and watch it repair itself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container play">
    <div class="card lab reveal">
      <SurfaceCodeLattice />
    </div>
    <aside class="how reveal">
      <h2>How to play</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Inject an error.</strong> Pick X (bit flip), Z (phase flip) or Y (both) and click a data qubit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read the syndrome.</strong> Neighbouring checks light up. You only see the checks, never the qubits, just
          like real hardware.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Decode.</strong> The decoder picks the smallest error consistent with the syndrome and proposes a
          correction (dashed rings).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Try to beat it.</strong> On d = 3, any single error is corrected. Now put two X errors in the same
          column (or two Z errors in the same row). The decoder blames a single qubit instead, and the "correction"
          completes a <em>logical error</em>. Half of all two-qubit X errors fool it this way.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="note">
        Under the hood: a rotated surface code with X and Z checks decoded independently. The decoder is an exact
        minimum-weight lookup table built by breadth-first search over syndromes. That works for codes this small; larger
        codes need matching (e.g. PyMatching) or learned decoders.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <CircuitDivider />

  <section class="section container">
    <p class="prompt">./threshold --noise bitflip --d 3 5</p>
    <h2>Does adding qubits help?</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Only if the hardware is good enough. Below a <strong>threshold</strong> error rate, a bigger code suppresses errors
      faster. Above it, more qubits just means more things to go wrong.
    </p>
    <div class="card reveal chart-card">
      <ThresholdChart />
    </div>
    <p class="caveat">
      Model: code capacity with independent bit flips and perfect syndrome measurement. That is the simplest setting, so
      these numbers are optimistic. With noisy measurements and circuit-level noise, thresholds drop to about 1%, which is
      why fast, accurate decoders matter so much.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container why">
    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="prompt">cat motivation.md</p>
      <h2>Why I built this</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        My research goal is <strong>machine-learning decoders that are accurate and fast enough for real-time error
        correction</strong>. A decoder has to turn a stream of syndromes into corrections faster than errors pile up.
        This playground is the smallest version of that problem.
      </p>
      <p class="lead">
        Next steps: circuit-level noise with <a href="https://github.com/quantumlib/Stim" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stim</a>,
        matching baselines with <a href="https://github.com/oscarhiggott/PyMatching" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PyMatching</a>,
        and comparing them against neural decoders trained on simulated syndromes.
      </p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={url('/research')}>My research →</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${profile.links.github}/my-website/blob/main/src/scripts/surfaceCode.ts`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the code ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .play {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.25fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 32px;
    align-items: start;
    padding-bottom: 48px;
  }
  .lab { padding: 24px 16px; }
  .how h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; }
  .how ol { padding-left: 1.2em; color: var(--text-dim); display: grid; gap: 12px; margin: 0 0 20px; }
  .how strong, .lead strong { color: var(--text); }
  .note, .caveat { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-dim); border-left: 2px solid var(--border); padding-left: 14px; }
  .caveat { margin-top: 20px; max-width: 75ch; }
  .chart-card { margin-top: 24px; }
  .why .lead { max-width: 70ch; }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; margin-top: 8px; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .play { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .lab, .chart-card { padding: 16px 10px; }
  }
</style>
